A boundary's `failed` slot lowers to a flag and a conditional, which is what
lets every target render the swap without a platform emitter growing a case:
passBoundaryFailed hangs `__failed0` on the owner, prepends the set to the
@error handler, and wraps the content in
`if __failed0 { FALLBACK } else { CONTENT }`.

The flag is marked Synthesized on the Var and on every reference to it, and the
html output is what holds the two together: a synthesized var is a top-level
binding and a synthesized reference is a bare name, so the page declares
`var __failed0__inst0` and reads it bare. Marked on one half only, it declared
that and read `state.__failed0__inst0` -- a different binding, falsy by
accident, and the fallback never showed. bubbletea says the same thing from the
other side: it skips the exported accessor pair for a synthesized field, and
`__failed0__inst0` title-cases to itself, so a method would have collided with
the field and the model would not have compiled.

deny out/none/html/index.html `state\.__failed0` -- a synthesized flag is a top-level binding, not a field of state
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `func \(m Model\) __failed0` -- no accessor: its name title-cases to itself and would collide with the field
deny * `component failed` -- the slot is gone by codegen; what survives is the conditional

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

component page() node {
    var seen = ""
    boundary(@error(e) {
        seen = e.message
    }) {
        button #go(text="Go", @click {
            error.raise("boom", "demo")
        })
        text(value=seen)

        component failed {
            text(value="it broke")
        }
    }
}

window #home(title="bf", href="/index.html") {
    page()
}

output {
    none {
        html
    }
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
}
